          %title Fields
          %h2#Fields Fields
          %p In this chapter we discuss these topics:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#FieldBundles"} Field bundles
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#NonFiniteDimensionalGeometry"} Spaces of field histories
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#InfinitesimalGeometry"} Infinitesimal geometry
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#Supergeometry"} Fermion fields and Supergeometry
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+history"} field history
            on a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            (a history of spatial
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+configurations"} field configurations
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldHistoriesAsHistoriesOfFieldConfigurations"}
            below) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantity"} quantity
            assigned to each point of spacetime (each
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/event"} event
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+history"} history
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Electromagnetism"}
            below) is at each point of spacetime a collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vectors"} vectors
            that encode the direction in which a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+particle"} charged particle
            passing through that point would feel a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+force"}> Lorentz force
            ”, see example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Electromagnetism"}
            below).
          %p
            This is readily formalized (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FieldsAndFieldBundles"}
            below): If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            of “values” that the given kind of field may take at any spacetime point, then a field history
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            is modeled as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            from spacetime to this space of values:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Phi
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\Sigma
                  \\longrightarrow
                  F
                  \\,.
          %p
            It will be useful to unify
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            and the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            values (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+fiber"} field fiber
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E \;\coloneqq\; \Sigma \times F
          %p
            and to think of this equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            map onto the first factor as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            of spaces of field values over spacetime
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo ×
                          %mi F
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi fb
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi pr
                                  %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  E &amp;\coloneqq&amp; \Sigma \times F
                  \\\
                  {}^{\mathllap{fb}}\downarrow &amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{pr_1}}
                  \\\
                  \\Sigma
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is then called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            of field values is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            (def.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FiberBundle"}
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+fiber"} field fiber
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles+in+physics"} fiber bundles in physics
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi fb
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \overset{fb}{\to}\Sigma
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+history"} field history
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of that bundle (def.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Sections"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            concerns the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of all possible field histories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+sections"} space of sections
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiffeologicalSpaceOfFieldHistories"}
            below). This is a very “large”
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiffeologicalSpace"}
            below).
          %p
            Or rather, in the presence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion+fields"} fermion fields
            such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiracFieldBundle"}
            below), the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+exclusion+principle"} Pauli exclusion principle
            demands that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifold"} super-manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiracSpaceOfFieldHistories"}
            below) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} super-geometric
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+smooth+set"} super smooth set
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SuperFormalSmoothSet"}
            below).
          %p
            This smooth structure on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            will be crucial when we discuss
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Observables"} below
