          %p
            The following example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FrechetManifoldsAreDiffeologicalSpaces"}
            is included only for readers who wonder how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+manifolds"} infinite-dimensional manifolds
            fit in. Since we will never actually use
            = succeed "-structure," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+manifold"} infinite-dimensional manifold
          #FrechetManifoldsAreDiffeologicalSpaces.num_example
            %h6#example_9 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifolds"} Fréchet manifolds
                are
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            %p
              Consider the particular type of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+manifolds"} infinite-dimensional manifolds
              called
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifolds"} Fréchet manifolds
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              are an example, for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifold"} Fréchet manifold
              there is a concept of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              the structure of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiffeologicalSpace"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              to be these smooth functions
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to X
            %p
              It turns out that then that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              two
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifolds"} Fréchet manifolds
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+bijection"} natural bijection
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
              between them regarded as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifolds"} Fréchet manifolds
              and [regarded as . Hence it does not matter which of the two perspectives we take (unless of course a  more general than a  enters the picture, at which point the second definition generalizes, whereas the first does not).]
            %p
              Stated more
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} abstractly
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifolds"} Fréchet manifolds
              form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              of that of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
              (
              %a{:href => "Fr&#233;chet+manifold#FFEmbeddingOfFrechetInDiffeological"}> this prop.
              ):
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FrechetManifolds
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi DiffeologicalSpaces
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    FrechetManifolds \hookrightarrow DiffeologicalSpaces
                    \\,.
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \simeq \Sigma \times F \to \Sigma
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+fiber+bundle"} trivial fiber bundle
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_\Sigma(E)
              carries a standard structure of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifold"} Fréchet manifold
              (see at
              = succeed ")." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+structure+of+mapping+spaces"} manifold structure of mapping spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifolds"} Fréchet manifolds
              into
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
              agrees with that from example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiffeologicalSpaceOfFieldHistories"}
              (see
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "Fr&#233;chet+manifold#CompatibilityWithDiffeologicalMappingSpaces"} this prop.
          %p
            Once the step from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiffeologicalSpace"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            of the space entirely by how we may probe it by mapping smooth Cartesian spaces into it, it becomes clear that the underlying set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_s
            of a diffeological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is not actually crucial to support the concept: The space is already entirely defined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structuralism"} structurally
            by the system of smooth plots it has, and the underlying set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_s
            is recovered from these as the set of plots from the point
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^0
          %p
            This is crucial for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+field+histories"} spaces of field histories
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermionic+fields"} fermionic fields
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FermionicBosonicFields"}
            below) such as the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiracSpaceOfFieldHistories"}
            below) do not have underlying sets of points the way
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            have. Informally, the reason is that a point is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic"} bosonic
            object, while and the nature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermionic+fields"} fermionic fields
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antimodal+type"} the opposite of
            bosonic.
          %p
            But we may just as well drop the mentioning of the underlying set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_s
            in the definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+spaces"} generalized smooth spaces
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiffeologicalSpace"}
            we obtain the following more general and more useful definition (still “bosonic”, though, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometric
            version is def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SuperFormalSmoothSet"}
            below):
          #SmoothSet.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
